---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../components/PageHeader.astro";
import PlaceholderBox from "../components/PlaceholderBox.astro";
import Disclaimer from "../components/Disclaimer.astro";
import { href } from "../site.config";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Project"
  description="The final-year project behind this site: problem, method, models, data, and limits, tied to the project report."
>
  <PageHeader
    title="The project"
    intro="AI-Driven Honeybee and Beehive Health Monitoring System for Disease Detection and Population Analysis. A final-year project, BSc Computer Science, Department of Computer Science, Namal University, cohort 2021–2025."
  />
  <div class="container">
    <p class="muted small">
      Most facts on this page come from the final-year project report, with the report section
      noted. The statistics section below cites its own sources. Where the report is silent, the
      page says so. Reported result figures are published with sources on the
      <a href={href("/research")}>Research</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={href("/images/stock/beehive_apiary.jpg")} alt="Beehives in an apiary" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>
        Illustrative image. Beehives in an apiary. Credits:
        <a href={href("/credits")}>Image credits</a>.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Honeybee decline threatens pollination and food security (Abstract; §1.1). The report argues
      that manual inspection is disruptive to colonies, slow, late in spotting disease, and hard to
      scale across many hives (§1.3). Inspectors also differ in experience, so results vary.
    </p>

    <h2>Why pollination matters — cited figures</h2>
    <p>
      These figures come from the sources named, not from the project report. Each figure states
      what it measures. All sources accessed 12 Nov 2026.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Figure</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Source</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>More than three quarters of the leading types of global food crops rely to some extent on animal pollination.</td>
            <td>Crop types (count), for yield and/or quality — dependence ranges from slight to essential.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 2</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Pollinator-dependent crops contribute 35% of global crop production volume.</td>
            <td>Production volume, not the count of crop types.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 2</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>5–8% of current global crop production, worth US$235–577 billion a year (2015 US$), is directly attributable to animal pollination.</td>
            <td>The production that would be lost without pollinators; the market value of added production, not the cost of replacing pollination.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 3</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Nearly 90% of wild flowering plant species depend at least in part on animal pollination.</td>
            <td>Wild plants, not crops.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 1</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Production volume of pollinator-dependent crops rose 300% over five decades.</td>
            <td>Volume growth to about 2016, making livelihoods more dependent on pollination.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 13</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>No global Red List assessment exists for insect pollinators. In Europe, 9% of bee species are threatened and populations are declining for 37% (57% data-deficient).</td>
            <td>A data gap, plus a European-scale assessment only — not a global rate.</td>
            <td><a href="https://files.ipbes.net/ipbes-web-prod-public-files/spm_deliverable_3a_pollination_20170222.pdf" rel="noopener">IPBES (2016), Key Message 12</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>16.4% of colonies lost over winter 2017/18 (95% CI 16.1–16.6%), ranging from 2.0% to 32.8% between countries.</td>
            <td>36 countries (33 European), 25,363 beekeepers, 544,879 colonies; queen problems, dead colonies, and natural disaster combined.</td>
            <td><a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/00218839.2019.1615661" rel="noopener">Gray et al. (2019), COLOSS survey</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>20.9% winter loss in 2016/17 (95% CI 20.6–21.3%).</td>
            <td>30 countries, 14,813 beekeepers, 425,762 colonies. The country group differs slightly from 2017/18.</td>
            <td><a href="https://strathprints.strath.ac.uk/64589/1/Brodschneider_etal_JAR_2018_Multi_country_loss_rates_of_honey_bee_colonies_during_winter.pdf" rel="noopener">Brodschneider et al. (2018), COLOSS survey</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>55.6% annual loss and 40.2% winter loss in the USA, 2024–25 (preliminary).</td>
            <td>2,453 beekeepers, 219,097 colonies; the highest losses since tracking began. Preliminary analysis, not yet peer-reviewed.</td>
            <td><a href="https://apiaryinspectors.org/US-beekeeping-survey-24-25/" rel="noopener">Giacobino et al., AIA survey</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Loss rates are mortality rates, not changes in total colony numbers: beekeepers replace lost
      colonies during the year. The European and US surveys use different methods and coverage, so
      their figures are not directly comparable.
    </p>
    <p>
      No colony-loss rate for Pakistan from an authoritative source was found, and no global
      colony-loss rate exists in these sources. None is shown.
    </p>

    <h2>Objectives</h2>
    <p>The report sets out objectives, not achieved results (§1.4):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Non-invasive, image-based monitoring of bee health and population.</li>
      <li>Early detection of Varroa mite infestation, Deformed Wing Virus (DWV), and Chalkbrood Disease.</li>
      <li>A simple interface usable on standard mobile devices, with no hardware installed in the hive.</li>
      <li>Fast, low-cost checks that a beekeeper can run in the field.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The report also lists numeric targets for detection quality, counting error, false alarms,
      availability, and daily volume (§1.4, Table 1.1). Those targets are not published as numbers
      on this site, because targets are not results. Reported results are published with sources on
      the <a href={href("/research")}>Research</a> page, and the demo models themselves are
      unmeasured. See <a href={href("/models-data")}>Models &amp; data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>The report describes a three-part framework (§4.1):</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Primary classification</strong> — is the image a bee or beehive image?</li>
      <li><strong>Health assessment</strong> — which of the three diseases, if any, is visible?</li>
      <li><strong>Population analysis</strong> — how many bees are in the image?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Image preparation in the report includes quality checks, enhancement, and standardisation to
      fixed input sizes (§4.3). Input guidance in the report covers common photo formats, a minimum
      size, and capture in natural daylight at close range (Table 4.1). The demo's own
      preprocessing follows the working inference code, not the report's training description; the
      two differ, and the parity test is the check that settles the demo behaviour. See
      <a href={href("/models-data")}>Models &amp; data</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The report's pipeline runs identification, disease checking, and counting as parallel paths
      and combines the results (§4.7). The website detector does not copy that flow: it uses a
      strict two-stage gate from the inference code, where Stage 2 runs only after Stage 1 passes.
      See <a href={href("/detector")}>Detector</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Models in the report — and in the demo</h2>
    <p>Keep these apart. The report describes more than the demo contains:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Model / approach</th><th>Role in the report</th><th>In the website demo?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>MobileNet (binary)</td>
            <td>Bee vs non-bee classification (§4.4).</td>
            <td>Yes — Stage 1 of the two supplied models.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>MobileNet (four classes)</td>
            <td>Health classification: Healthy, Varroa, Deformed Wing Disease, Chalkbrood (§4.5).</td>
            <td>Yes — Stage 2 of the two supplied models. Labels as in the labels files.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>YOLO-based bee counting (research)</td>
            <td>Population counting from hive images (§4.6).</td>
            <td>No. No counting model was supplied. Planned work only; see Roadmap.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ResNet</td>
            <td>Literature review and one baseline comparison row (Table 5.4).</td>
            <td>No. Baseline and literature only.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>CNN (general)</td>
            <td>Mainly literature review; one behaviour-monitoring path is mentioned.</td>
            <td>No. Literature only.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small muted">
      No counting-model version number is shown anywhere on this site, per the project lead's
      decision. The counting work is described only as YOLO-based bee counting (research).
    </p>

    <h2>Data</h2>
    <p>
      The report describes three data sources (§9.1): public datasets, web-collected material, and
      field photos and videos from apiaries. No dataset names, links, or licences are given, and no
      apiary is named. Dataset sizes are stated per model in Chapters 4 and 11 and do not reconcile
      into one total; they are described per model on the
      <a href={href("/models-data")}>Models &amp; data</a> page, with section citations, and are not
      presented as verified training data for the two supplied model files.
    </p>
    <p>
      Training and test images are not republished on this site. Licences and consent for the
      collected images are unknown. See <a href={href("/models-data")}>Models &amp; data</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Prototype described in the report</h2>
    <p>
      The report describes both a cloud-style design (Chapter 7) and a localhost prototype with no
      cloud services (Chapter 11). This website is a new static build and is neither of those. It
      reuses the report's facts and the two supplied models only. A mobile app is described in the
      report's future work (Chapter 13) as planned; no app is linked or offered here.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits, in the report's own terms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Visual-only checking misses early or internal disease with no visible sign (§6.3.1).
      </li>
      <li>
        Testing stayed mainly in laboratory and controlled settings. Performance in working apiaries
        over full seasons was largely untested (§6.3.3, §6.5.1).
      </li>
      <li>
        The report's final testing verdict is prototype: suitable for demonstration and limited
        trials with beekeepers who understand its prototype nature (§11.10.3).
      </li>
      <li>
        Small expert checks in the report gave mixed agreement, weakest on subtle and early-stage
        cases (§11.8). The reported figures are published with sources on the
        <a href={href("/research")}>Research</a> page.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <PlaceholderBox text="journey video and project photos, with rights and permissions confirmed" />

    <Disclaimer />

